From 5d35c6dd6c2834ee58545fd2ad28a5407c51e979 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 14:33:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] Take the views of a stereoscopic 3D video apart in
 VideoOutput

A QML player had to know every packing of a stereoscopic 3D video, take the
views apart and shape them itself, as Dragon Player and Plank Player did with a
filter each of about 700 lines. QVideoFrameFormat::stereoMode() now says how
the frames of a video hold the two views, and VideoOutput can act on it.

New properties of VideoOutput (and Video):
 - stereoInput: the packing the frames have, Automatic from the stream, or
   said for a video that does not say it (every packing of H.264 Table D-8 and
   H.265 Table D.8, which RFC 9559 shares, side by side and top and bottom
   half or full size, the anaglyphs shown as they are, or not 3D);
 - stereoSwapEyes: the first and second view the other way round;
 - stereoView: both eyes, the left or the right eye only, or the frame as
   stored. Only one view fits the window: side by side and top and bottom
   show their part of the frame (any depth), the interleaved packings, the
   checkerboard and the frame sequence are taken apart on the GPU's float
   image of the frame and shown from there;
 - stereoShapeFallback: a video that says nothing and is at least 2.5 times as
   wide as high is side by side, full size, the last resort after the stream;
 - stereoScaling (bilinear, bicubic, Lanczos) restores the size of a half view
   or of an interleaved one; stereoSurface is the depth of a surface the views
   are shown in where there is one of their own;
 - stereoVideo, stereoPacking, stereoLayerSupported, stereoLayerActive: what
   the video in hand is and how it is shown.

A frame of a frame sequence says its eye only when the views of consecutive
frames differ; one stream-wide value (Matroska's tag) is not taken for it, the
frames then come in turns, the first the first eye's, as the stream says.

qImageFromVideoFrame() gets a floating point depth: the colour matrix is
applied to the samples of a high bit depth video, on the GPU, without rounding
them to 8 bits, and PQ and HLG video keeps its code values. Where there is no
GPU to render to floating point the 8 bit image is returned.

Defaults are what Qt does without any of it: the frame as it is stored, bilinear,
an 8 bit surface. A platform that can show both views to their eyes (a
compositor that takes the stereo content of a surface, compiled in with
QT_MM_STEREO_LAYER) is a separate change.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet via Claude Code
